<Doc title="An LED on a GPIO" subtitle="resistor, polarity, and why backwards looks like broken" category="Circuits" id="ledOnAGpio">

<Table>
<Row head="yes"><Cell>part</Cell><Cell>Pico</Cell><Cell>what it is</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>long leg, anode (+)</Cell><Cell class="digital">GP3</Cell><Cell>physical pin 5, through the resistor</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>short leg, cathode (-)</Cell><Cell class="ground">GND</Cell><Cell>physical pin 3</Cell></Row>
<Row><Cell>resistor</Cell><Cell></Cell><Cell>220 ohm - 1k; red red brown = 220</Cell></Row>
</Table>

<Draw width="640" height="190">
# The Pico's edge. A body is drawn as its outline and nothing else: this page
# is a wireframe, and a solid slate rectangle is a picture of a board rather
# than a drawing of one.
setColor grey
setWidth 1
drawRect 30 20 108 172
setColor white
setTextSize 11
drawText 48 29 "Pico 2 W"

# ---- GP3, physical pin 5. Green is a free GPIO.
setColor green
fillRect 100 88 118 104
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 12
drawText 77 90 "GP3"
setColor grey
setTextSize 10
drawText 70 106 "pin 5"

# ---- GND, physical pin 3.
setColor grey
fillRect 100 144 118 160
setColor brWhite
setTextSize 12
drawText 77 146 "GND"
setColor grey
setTextSize 10
drawText 70 162 "pin 3"

# ---- GPIO out to the resistor.
setColor grey
setWidth 2
drawLine 118 96 190 96

# The resistor, drawn WITH its bands, because reading them off the part is half
# the skill and the colours are on the sibling page.
setColor yellow
setWidth 1
drawRect 190 83 268 109

# The three bands are NOT taken from the sixteen, deliberately. These are the
# resistor colour CODE, a real-world standard, and a brown band that is not
# brown is simply wrong. The palette governs the interface, not the physics it
# depicts.
setColor #C0392B
fillRect 204 83 211 109
setColor #C0392B
fillRect 219 83 226 109
setColor #8B5A2B
fillRect 234 83 241 109

setColor brWhite
setTextSize 11
drawText 205 64 "220R - 1k"
setColor grey
setTextSize 10
drawText 183 119 "red red brown = 220"

# ---- resistor out to the LED.
setColor grey
setWidth 2
drawLine 268 96 340 96

# The LED: a triangle plus a bar, with the legs drawn to LENGTH, because the
# long leg is the entire orientation cue on a real part.
setColor red
setWidth 1
drawTriangle 340 78 340 114 372 96
setWidth 3
drawLine 372 78 372 114

setColor brWhite
setTextSize 11
drawText 348 60 "LED"
setColor green
setTextSize 10
drawText 290 125 "long leg (anode, +)"
setColor white
drawText 361 125 "short leg (cathode, -)"

# ---- cathode back down to ground.
setColor grey
setWidth 2
drawLine 372 96 470 96
drawLine 470 96 470 152
drawLine 470 152 118 152

# Current direction, so the drawing says which way round it all goes.
setColor grey
setTextSize 10
drawText 529 91 "current flows"
drawText 536 105 "GP3 -> GND"
</Draw>

<Warn><Bold>Backwards is not broken - it is dark, which looks exactly like a
dead pin.</Bold> An LED only conducts one way, and fitted the wrong way round it
is undamaged and simply never lights.</Warn>

<Note><Bold>The resistor may sit on either side of the LED.</Bold> Without one
the LED is close to a short across a 3.3 V pin, which risks both parts.</Note>

</Doc>
